(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("reliability-practice",{"reliability-practice/choosing-slis-by-service-type":{"html":"\u003cp>An SLO is only as good as its indicator. A number that is easy to collect but does not match user experience gives false comfort, so choose indicators by asking what a user would call broken.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What makes a good SLI\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Indicators for request driven services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Indicators for data pipelines, storage and batch jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where to measure: server, balancer, client or probe\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Good events, valid events and the ratio\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-makes-a-good-sli\">What makes a good SLI\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A good indicator is a ratio of good events to valid events, correlates with user happiness, and can be measured reliably. It moves when users are hurt and stays still when they are not. CPU use fails that test, because high CPU is often fine. Success rate and latency of real requests pass it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"request-services\">Request services\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For a request service use availability, the share of valid requests that succeed, and latency, the share served faster than a threshold. Use a threshold at a chosen percentile, such as ninety nine percent of requests under three hundred milliseconds. Count client errors as valid events only where they reflect a failure of the service.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pipelines-and-storage\">Pipelines and storage\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For a data pipeline use freshness, the share of time that data is newer than a limit, and correctness, the share of records that pass checks. For storage use durability, the share of objects retrievable intact, and read and write success. For batch jobs use the share of runs that finish in time with correct output.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-to-measure\">Where to measure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Server side metrics are easy but miss failures before the request arrives. Load balancer logs see more of the path. Client side or real user measurements see the most but are noisy. Synthetic probes give a steady signal but test only what they call. Combine two sources and prefer the one closest to the user that you trust.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"good-valid-and-the-ratio\">Good, valid and the ratio\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Define which events are valid and which are good in words first. Exclude health checks and known test traffic from valid events. Write the query that counts both sides, and test it with a failure. A clear definition lets everyone calculate the same number and makes disputes short.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"user&gt;sli;sli&gt;src;src&gt;slo\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Choosing SLIs by service type\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"user\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">User journey\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sli\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">SLI definition\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"src\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Measurement source\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"slo\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Target\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, User journey to SLI definition; 2, SLI definition to Measurement source; 3, Measurement source to Target.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"user\">\u003cdt>User journey \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> What feels broken.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not asked.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sli\">\u003cdt>SLI definition \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Good over valid.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Vague.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"src\">\u003cdt>Measurement source \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Balancer or client.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Blind spot.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"slo\">\u003cdt>Target \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Goal over a window.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too strict.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-green-dashboards-and-angry-customers\">Incident: Green dashboards and angry customers\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The availability dashboard showed one hundred percent while customers could not check out.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s -o /dev/null -w &quot;%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n&quot; https://shop.example.com/checkout\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -o /dev/null -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n&quot;\u003c/span> https://shop.example.com/checkout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">200 11.482\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The indicator counted only the share of responses that were not server errors, and the checkout page returned 200 after eleven seconds, so it counted as good. Customers abandoned the page. The team added a latency threshold to the indicator, measured it at the load balancer for the checkout journey and set an alert on the new burn rate. The dashboard then matched the experience.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using CPU or memory as an SLI\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Counting a slow success as good\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Including health checks in the denominator\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring only inside the service\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Defining indicators without asking users\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-makes-a-good-sli","text":"What makes a good SLI"},{"id":"request-services","text":"Request services"},{"id":"pipelines-and-storage","text":"Pipelines and storage"},{"id":"where-to-measure","text":"Where to measure"},{"id":"good-valid-and-the-ratio","text":"Good, valid and the ratio"},{"id":"incident-green-dashboards-and-angry-customers","text":"Incident: Green dashboards and angry customers"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Service Level Objectives","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/"},{"t":"SRE Workbook: implementing SLOs","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/"},{"t":"Practical alerting","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/practical-alerting/"}],"text":"An SLO is only as good as its indicator. A number that is easy to collect but does not match user experience gives false comfort, so choose indicators by asking what a user would call broken. What you will learn What makes a good SLI Indicators for request driven services Indicators for data pipelines, storage and batch jobs Where to measure: server, balancer, client or probe Good events, valid events and the ratio What makes a good SLI A good indicator is a ratio of good events to valid events, correlates with user happiness, and can be measured reliably. It moves when users are hurt and stays still when they are not. CPU use fails that test, because high CPU is often fine. Success rate and latency of real requests pass it. Request services For a request service use availability, the share of valid requests that succeed, and latency, the share served faster than a threshold. Use a threshold at a chosen percentile, such as ninety nine percent of requests under three hundred milliseconds. Count client errors as valid events only where they reflect a failure of the service. Pipelines and storage For a data pipeline use freshness, the share of time that data is newer than a limit, and correctness, the share of records that pass checks. For storage use durability, the share of objects retrievable intact, and read and write success. For batch jobs use the share of runs that finish in time with correct output. Where to measure Server side metrics are easy but miss failures before the request arrives. Load balancer logs see more of the path. Client side or real user measurements see the most but are noisy. Synthetic probes give a steady signal but test only what they call. Combine two sources and prefer the one closest to the user that you trust. Good, valid and the ratio Define which events are valid and which are good in words first. Exclude health checks and known test traffic from valid events. Write the query that counts both sides, and test it with a failure. A clear definition lets everyone calculate the same number and makes disputes short. Choosing SLIs by service type Flow in order: 1, User journey to SLI definition; 2, SLI definition to Measurement source; 3, Measurement source to Target. Outside party Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. User journey Outside party What it does: What feels broken. If it fails or is weak: Not asked. SLI definition Step What it does: Good over valid. If it fails or is weak: Vague. Measurement source Observability What it does: Balancer or client. If it fails or is weak: Blind spot. Target Step What it does: Goal over a window. If it fails or is weak: Too strict. Incident: Green dashboards and angry customers Incident The availability dashboard showed one hundred percent while customers could not check out. $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n\" https://shop.example.com/checkout 200 11.482 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The indicator counted only the share of responses that were not server errors, and the checkout page returned 200 after eleven seconds, so it counted as good. Customers abandoned the page. The team added a latency threshold to the indicator, measured it at the load balancer for the checkout journey and set an alert on the new burn rate. The dashboard then matched the experience. Common mistakes Using CPU or memory as an SLI Counting a slow success as good Including health checks in the denominator Measuring only inside the service Defining indicators without asking users"},"reliability-practice/running-an-slo-workshop-and-writing-an-error-budget-policy":{"html":"\u003cp>An SLO that nobody agreed to and no policy that says what to do about it is only a chart. The workshop and the policy turn numbers into decisions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Who must be in the room\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Workshop steps from journeys to targets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing a target from history and need\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing the error budget policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reviewing and changing SLOs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"who-is-in-the-room\">Who is in the room\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Include the product owner who knows what matters to users, engineers who know how the service fails, the on call lead and a stakeholder who can accept trade offs. Without the product owner the targets are technical, and without an engineer they are fantasy. Keep the group small and send the data in advance.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"workshop-steps\">Workshop steps\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>List the critical user journeys. For each, write what broken looks like. Propose indicators and check that data exists. Look at the last quarter of measurements to see what was achieved. Draft a target and a window, then discuss what a miss would cost the users and the business. End with owners and a date for the first review.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing-a-target\">Choosing a target\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start from what users need and what the service has achieved, and not from a round number. Ninety nine point nine percent allows about forty three minutes of failure in a month, while ninety nine point ninety nine allows about four. Each extra nine costs more and slows change. A dependency with a lower target limits what you can promise.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-policy\">The policy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write down what happens when the budget is spent. For example: when more than half is used, review risky changes; when it is used up, pause non critical releases and work on reliability until it recovers; if one incident used most of it, require a post incident review with actions. Get product and engineering leaders to sign it, so that it is applied without a debate each time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"review\">Review\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Review SLOs every quarter. Tighten if users are unhappy despite meeting the target, relax if the target is costly and users do not notice. Record the changes with the reason. Retire SLOs that nobody uses. The policy should have exceptions that are named, such as security fixes, so that it is not ignored in an emergency.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"wk&gt;tgt;tgt&gt;pol;pol&gt;rev\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Running an SLO workshop and writing an error budget policy\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wk\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Workshop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tgt\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Targets\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pol\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Budget policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rev\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Quarterly review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Workshop to Targets; 2, Targets to Budget policy; 3, Budget policy to Quarterly review.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wk\">\u003cdt>Workshop \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Journeys and data.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No product owner.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tgt\">\u003cdt>Targets \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> From history and need.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Round number.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pol\">\u003cdt>Budget policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Signed rules.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignored.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rev\">\u003cdt>Quarterly review \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tighten or relax.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-slo-that-nobody-could-use\">Incident: The SLO that nobody could use\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team met its SLO every month, but release pressure continued and reliability work never got time.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat slo-policy.md | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> slo-policy.md | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"># Error budget policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Status: draft, not yet approved\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Owner: unassigned\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The SLO existed but the policy had never been approved, so a low budget changed nothing. When a major incident used most of the budget, the next release went ahead anyway. The team ran a workshop with the product owner, wrote a short policy with clear triggers, got it signed by the engineering and product directors, and applied it in the next quarter when the budget fell below half.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No product owner in the workshop\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A target chosen as a round number\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A policy that is never approved\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding nines without cost analysis\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never reviewing the SLOs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"who-is-in-the-room","text":"Who is in the room"},{"id":"workshop-steps","text":"Workshop steps"},{"id":"choosing-a-target","text":"Choosing a target"},{"id":"the-policy","text":"The policy"},{"id":"review","text":"Review"},{"id":"incident-the-slo-that-nobody-could-use","text":"Incident: The SLO that nobody could use"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"SRE Workbook: implementing SLOs","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/"},{"t":"SRE Workbook: error budget policy","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/error-budget-policy/"},{"t":"Service Level Objectives","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/"}],"text":"An SLO that nobody agreed to and no policy that says what to do about it is only a chart. The workshop and the policy turn numbers into decisions. What you will learn Who must be in the room Workshop steps from journeys to targets Choosing a target from history and need Writing the error budget policy Reviewing and changing SLOs Who is in the room Include the product owner who knows what matters to users, engineers who know how the service fails, the on call lead and a stakeholder who can accept trade offs. Without the product owner the targets are technical, and without an engineer they are fantasy. Keep the group small and send the data in advance. Workshop steps List the critical user journeys. For each, write what broken looks like. Propose indicators and check that data exists. Look at the last quarter of measurements to see what was achieved. Draft a target and a window, then discuss what a miss would cost the users and the business. End with owners and a date for the first review. Choosing a target Start from what users need and what the service has achieved, and not from a round number. Ninety nine point nine percent allows about forty three minutes of failure in a month, while ninety nine point ninety nine allows about four. Each extra nine costs more and slows change. A dependency with a lower target limits what you can promise. The policy Write down what happens when the budget is spent. For example: when more than half is used, review risky changes; when it is used up, pause non critical releases and work on reliability until it recovers; if one incident used most of it, require a post incident review with actions. Get product and engineering leaders to sign it, so that it is applied without a debate each time. Review Review SLOs every quarter. Tighten if users are unhappy despite meeting the target, relax if the target is costly and users do not notice. Record the changes with the reason. Retire SLOs that nobody uses. The policy should have exceptions that are named, such as security fixes, so that it is not ignored in an emergency. Running an SLO workshop and writing an error budget policy Flow in order: 1, Workshop to Targets; 2, Targets to Budget policy; 3, Budget policy to Quarterly review. Step Security Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Workshop Step What it does: Journeys and data. If it fails or is weak: No product owner. Targets Step What it does: From history and need. If it fails or is weak: Round number. Budget policy Security What it does: Signed rules. If it fails or is weak: Ignored. Quarterly review Observability What it does: Tighten or relax. If it fails or is weak: Skipped. Incident: The SLO that nobody could use Incident A team met its SLO every month, but release pressure continued and reliability work never got time. $ cat slo-policy.md | head -3 # Error budget policy Status: draft, not yet approved Owner: unassigned (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The SLO existed but the policy had never been approved, so a low budget changed nothing. When a major incident used most of the budget, the next release went ahead anyway. The team ran a workshop with the product owner, wrote a short policy with clear triggers, got it signed by the engineering and product directors, and applied it in the next quarter when the budget fell below half. Common mistakes No product owner in the workshop A target chosen as a round number A policy that is never approved Adding nines without cost analysis Never reviewing the SLOs"},"reliability-practice/alert-quality-and-on-call-health":{"html":"\u003cp>People are part of the system. A rotation that is woken every night makes mistakes and leaves, and that is a reliability risk as real as a failing disk.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Measuring pages: volume, actionability and timing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Classifying alerts: page, ticket or delete\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Burn rate alerts and symptom based paging\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>On call load, fairness and recovery\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handover and follow the sun options\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measure-pages\">Measure pages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For each alert count how often it fired, how often it needed action, how long it took to resolve and when it fired. Pages at night weigh more than pages in the day. Review the numbers weekly with the rotation. An alert that fires often and needs no action is the first candidate to change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"page-ticket-or-delete\">Page, ticket or delete\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A page means a human must act now to protect users. A ticket means work is needed but can wait. Anything else is a dashboard or should be deleted. Apply the test to each alert: what will the responder do, is it urgent and does it relate to user impact. Many cause based alerts fail the test.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"symptom-based-paging\">Symptom based paging\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Page on symptoms users feel, such as error budget burn, and keep cause alerts as supporting information for diagnosis. A high burn rate means users are hurt now. A disk at eighty percent is a ticket. Link every page to a runbook and a dashboard so that the responder starts with context.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"load-and-fairness\">Load and fairness\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set a target such as at most two actionable pages per shift and track against it. Share the load fairly, pay or compensate for on call, and give time to recover after a night incident. Track the number of people who can take the rotation and the shadowing of new members, since a thin rotation is fragile.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"handover\">Handover\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write a short handover note with open issues, recent changes and risky times. Consider follow the sun coverage across time zones when the load requires it. Review the rotation health each quarter with the team and management, and treat a bad trend as a reliability problem that needs funding.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"data&gt;cls;cls&gt;sym;sym&gt;health\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Alert quality and on call health\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"data\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Page statistics\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cls\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Classify alerts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sym\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Symptom paging\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"health\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">On call health\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Page statistics to Classify alerts; 2, Classify alerts to Symptom paging; 3, Symptom paging to On call health.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"data\">\u003cdt>Page statistics \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Count and timing.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unknown.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cls\">\u003cdt>Classify alerts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Page, ticket, delete.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> All pages.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sym\">\u003cdt>Symptom paging \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Burn rate.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cause noise.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"health\">\u003cdt>On call health \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Load and recovery.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Burnout.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-three-pages-a-night-for-a-month\">Incident: Three pages a night for a month\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A small team lost two engineers after a month of frequent night pages.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat pages-last-30-days.csv | awk -F, 'NR&gt;1 {c[$2]++} END {for (k in c) print c[k], k}' | sort -rn | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> pages-last-30-days.csv | awk -F, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;NR&gt;1 {c[$2]++} END {for (k in c) print c[k], k}&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -rn | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">61 DiskUsageHigh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">44 PodRestartedOnce\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">29 CpuAbove80\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The three noisiest alerts were cause based and needed no action most of the time. The team moved the disk and CPU alerts to tickets with trend based predictions, replaced the restart alert with a burn rate page and set a target of two actionable pages per shift. Night pages fell by eighty percent in six weeks, and the next quarterly survey showed better on call satisfaction.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Paging on causes that need no action\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No measurement of pages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unfair rotation load\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No time to recover after night work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alerts with no runbook link\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"measure-pages","text":"Measure pages"},{"id":"page-ticket-or-delete","text":"Page, ticket or delete"},{"id":"symptom-based-paging","text":"Symptom based paging"},{"id":"load-and-fairness","text":"Load and fairness"},{"id":"handover","text":"Handover"},{"id":"incident-three-pages-a-night-for-a-month","text":"Incident: Three pages a night for a month"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"SRE Workbook: alerting on SLOs","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/alerting-on-slos/"},{"t":"SRE book: being on call","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/being-on-call/"},{"t":"Practical alerting","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/practical-alerting/"}],"text":"People are part of the system. A rotation that is woken every night makes mistakes and leaves, and that is a reliability risk as real as a failing disk. What you will learn Measuring pages: volume, actionability and timing Classifying alerts: page, ticket or delete Burn rate alerts and symptom based paging On call load, fairness and recovery Handover and follow the sun options Measure pages For each alert count how often it fired, how often it needed action, how long it took to resolve and when it fired. Pages at night weigh more than pages in the day. Review the numbers weekly with the rotation. An alert that fires often and needs no action is the first candidate to change. Page, ticket or delete A page means a human must act now to protect users. A ticket means work is needed but can wait. Anything else is a dashboard or should be deleted. Apply the test to each alert: what will the responder do, is it urgent and does it relate to user impact. Many cause based alerts fail the test. Symptom based paging Page on symptoms users feel, such as error budget burn, and keep cause alerts as supporting information for diagnosis. A high burn rate means users are hurt now. A disk at eighty percent is a ticket. Link every page to a runbook and a dashboard so that the responder starts with context. Load and fairness Set a target such as at most two actionable pages per shift and track against it. Share the load fairly, pay or compensate for on call, and give time to recover after a night incident. Track the number of people who can take the rotation and the shadowing of new members, since a thin rotation is fragile. Handover Write a short handover note with open issues, recent changes and risky times. Consider follow the sun coverage across time zones when the load requires it. Review the rotation health each quarter with the team and management, and treat a bad trend as a reliability problem that needs funding. Alert quality and on call health Flow in order: 1, Page statistics to Classify alerts; 2, Classify alerts to Symptom paging; 3, Symptom paging to On call health. Observability Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Page statistics Observability What it does: Count and timing. If it fails or is weak: Unknown. Classify alerts Step What it does: Page, ticket, delete. If it fails or is weak: All pages. Symptom paging Step What it does: Burn rate. If it fails or is weak: Cause noise. On call health Service What it does: Load and recovery. If it fails or is weak: Burnout. Incident: Three pages a night for a month Incident A small team lost two engineers after a month of frequent night pages. $ cat pages-last-30-days.csv | awk -F, &#x27;NR>1 {c[$2]++} END {for (k in c) print c[k], k}&#x27; | sort -rn | head -3 61 DiskUsageHigh 44 PodRestartedOnce 29 CpuAbove80 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The three noisiest alerts were cause based and needed no action most of the time. The team moved the disk and CPU alerts to tickets with trend based predictions, replaced the restart alert with a burn rate page and set a target of two actionable pages per shift. Night pages fell by eighty percent in six weeks, and the next quarterly survey showed better on call satisfaction. Common mistakes Paging on causes that need no action No measurement of pages Unfair rotation load No time to recover after night work Alerts with no runbook link"},"reliability-practice/tabletops-and-game-days-how-to-facilitate":{"html":"\u003cp>Teams learn the weak points of their response before a real incident only if they practise. A good exercise is safe, focused and ends with changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Tabletop versus live game day\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing a scenario and the injects\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Roles: facilitator, scribe, observers and responders\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safety: scope, abort rules and communication\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>After the exercise: findings with owners and dates\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-formats\">Two formats\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A tabletop is a discussion of a scenario with no systems touched, and it tests decisions, roles and communication. A game day injects a real failure, such as stopping a dependency in a controlled environment or in production with a small blast radius, and tests the system and the people together. Start with tabletops and move to live exercises as confidence grows.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scenario-and-injects\">Scenario and injects\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pick a realistic and specific failure, such as the primary database loses its leader during a sale. Write a timeline of injects, which are new facts given to the team at set times, such as an alert, a customer message or a failed fix. Include a complication, for example the runbook step is out of date, because that is where learning happens.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roles\">Roles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The facilitator runs the clock and gives injects. A scribe records the timeline, decisions and questions. Observers watch the response, communication and tools. Responders act as they would in a real incident, with their normal on call roles. The facilitator must not help the responders solve the scenario, or the exercise shows nothing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safety\">Safety\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Agree the scope, the systems that may be touched and an abort word that anyone can use. For live exercises, have a rollback ready, tell stakeholders and support teams, and avoid periods of high risk. Monitor customer impact during the exercise and stop if it exceeds the limit. Never run a surprise exercise in production without leadership approval.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"follow-up\">Follow up\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Hold a short review straight after, then write findings as actions with an owner and a date, such as update the runbook, add an alert or practise the failover again. Track them like other work and check progress at the next exercise. Share a summary with the wider organisation to spread what was learned.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"plan&gt;run;run&gt;safe;safe&gt;act\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Tabletops and game days: how to facilitate\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"plan\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scenario and injects\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"run\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Exercise\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"safe\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Safety rules\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"act\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Findings and actions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Scenario and injects to Exercise; 2, Exercise to Safety rules; 3, Safety rules to Findings and actions.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"plan\">\u003cdt>Scenario and injects \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Realistic and specific.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Vague.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"run\">\u003cdt>Exercise \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Facilitator and scribe.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rescued.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"safe\">\u003cdt>Safety rules \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Abort and scope.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> None.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"act\">\u003cdt>Findings and actions \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Owners and dates.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Lost.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-runbook-that-pointed-to-a-deleted-server\">Incident: The runbook that pointed to a deleted server\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A game day showed that the failover step named a host that no longer existed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -n &quot;failover&quot; runbooks/db-primary.md | head -2\ngetent hosts db-old-03.example.internal\necho $?\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;failover&quot;\u003c/span> runbooks/db-primary.md | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">17: ssh db-old-03.example.internal and run promote.sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>getent hosts db-old-03.example.internal\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> $?\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The scribe recorded that responders lost eight minutes before they found the current procedure. The facilitator did not rescue them. In the review the team updated the runbook, added a test that checks that every host in a runbook resolves, and booked a repeat exercise for the next quarter. The second run took two minutes for the same step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A facilitator who solves the problem for the team\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No abort rule\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Surprise exercises with no approval\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No scribe so nothing is recorded\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Findings without owners\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"two-formats","text":"Two formats"},{"id":"scenario-and-injects","text":"Scenario and injects"},{"id":"roles","text":"Roles"},{"id":"safety","text":"Safety"},{"id":"follow-up","text":"Follow up"},{"id":"incident-the-runbook-that-pointed-to-a-deleted-server","text":"Incident: The runbook that pointed to a deleted server"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Chaos engineering principles","u":"https://principlesofchaos.org/"},{"t":"Disaster role playing","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/accelerating-sre-on-call/"},{"t":"Postmortem culture","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/"}],"text":"Teams learn the weak points of their response before a real incident only if they practise. A good exercise is safe, focused and ends with changes. What you will learn Tabletop versus live game day Writing a scenario and the injects Roles: facilitator, scribe, observers and responders Safety: scope, abort rules and communication After the exercise: findings with owners and dates Two formats A tabletop is a discussion of a scenario with no systems touched, and it tests decisions, roles and communication. A game day injects a real failure, such as stopping a dependency in a controlled environment or in production with a small blast radius, and tests the system and the people together. Start with tabletops and move to live exercises as confidence grows. Scenario and injects Pick a realistic and specific failure, such as the primary database loses its leader during a sale. Write a timeline of injects, which are new facts given to the team at set times, such as an alert, a customer message or a failed fix. Include a complication, for example the runbook step is out of date, because that is where learning happens. Roles The facilitator runs the clock and gives injects. A scribe records the timeline, decisions and questions. Observers watch the response, communication and tools. Responders act as they would in a real incident, with their normal on call roles. The facilitator must not help the responders solve the scenario, or the exercise shows nothing. Safety Agree the scope, the systems that may be touched and an abort word that anyone can use. For live exercises, have a rollback ready, tell stakeholders and support teams, and avoid periods of high risk. Monitor customer impact during the exercise and stop if it exceeds the limit. Never run a surprise exercise in production without leadership approval. Follow up Hold a short review straight after, then write findings as actions with an owner and a date, such as update the runbook, add an alert or practise the failover again. Track them like other work and check progress at the next exercise. Share a summary with the wider organisation to spread what was learned. Tabletops and game days: how to facilitate Flow in order: 1, Scenario and injects to Exercise; 2, Exercise to Safety rules; 3, Safety rules to Findings and actions. Step Service Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Scenario and injects Step What it does: Realistic and specific. If it fails or is weak: Vague. Exercise Service What it does: Facilitator and scribe. If it fails or is weak: Rescued. Safety rules Security What it does: Abort and scope. If it fails or is weak: None. Findings and actions Step What it does: Owners and dates. If it fails or is weak: Lost. Incident: The runbook that pointed to a deleted server Incident A game day showed that the failover step named a host that no longer existed. $ grep -n \"failover\" runbooks/db-primary.md | head -2 17: ssh db-old-03.example.internal and run promote.sh $ getent hosts db-old-03.example.internal $ echo $? 2 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The scribe recorded that responders lost eight minutes before they found the current procedure. The facilitator did not rescue them. In the review the team updated the runbook, added a test that checks that every host in a runbook resolves, and booked a repeat exercise for the next quarter. The second run took two minutes for the same step. Common mistakes A facilitator who solves the problem for the team No abort rule Surprise exercises with no approval No scribe so nothing is recorded Findings without owners"},"reliability-practice/testing-resilience-in-pipelines-load-soak-and-fault-injection":{"html":"\u003cp>Reliability that is only tested in production is tested by users. Many failure modes can be found earlier with repeatable tests that run for every release.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What each test type finds\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Load tests with realistic traffic shapes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Soak tests for leaks and slow decay\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fault injection: latency, errors and dependency loss\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Release gates and canary analysis\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-types\">Test types\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A load test shows how the service behaves at expected and peak traffic. A stress test finds the breaking point. A soak test runs for hours or days to find leaks and slow decay. A fault injection test breaks a dependency or adds latency to check that fallbacks work. Each answers a different question, so choose by the risk.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"load-tests\">Load tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use traffic that resembles production in mix and shape, not a single endpoint at constant rate. Replay anonymised traffic or model the real mix, including bursts. Measure latency percentiles and errors, as well as resource use, and compare against the last release. A trend over releases finds regressions early.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"soak-tests\">Soak tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run the service at steady load for a long time and watch memory, file descriptors, connection counts and latency over time. A slow rise points to a leak. Soak tests also catch problems with log rotation, certificate renewal and scheduled jobs that appear only after hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fault-injection\">Fault injection\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Add delay or errors to a dependency call, stop a replica, or block a network path, and check that timeouts, retries, circuit breakers and fallbacks do what the design says. Begin in staging, then run small experiments in production with a clear stop condition. Record the hypothesis, the result and the fix.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"gates-and-canaries\">Gates and canaries\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use the SLO signals as gates: a canary release receives a small share of traffic, and the pipeline compares its error rate and latency with the baseline. If it is worse, the release stops or rolls back automatically. Keep the gate fast and the thresholds simple, so that people trust it and do not bypass it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"ci&gt;load;load&gt;fault;fault&gt;gate\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Testing resilience in pipelines: load, soak and fault injection\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ci\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pipeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"load\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Load and soak\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fault\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fault injection\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gate\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Canary gate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pipeline to Load and soak; 2, Load and soak to Fault injection; 3, Fault injection to Canary gate.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ci\">\u003cdt>Pipeline \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Every release.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"load\">\u003cdt>Load and soak \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shape and time.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unrealistic.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fault\">\u003cdt>Fault injection \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Break a dependency.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No fallback.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gate\">\u003cdt>Canary gate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> SLO comparison.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Bypassed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-leak-that-only-showed-on-day-three\">Incident: The leak that only showed on day three\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A service that passed every test ran out of file descriptors three days after release.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls /proc/$(pgrep -o app)/fd | wc -l\nulimit -n\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /proc/$(pgrep -o app)/fd | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">31872\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ulimit\u003c/span> -n\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">32768\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The tests ran for ten minutes and the leak of one descriptor per slow request needed days to show. A soak test of twelve hours at steady load, which the team added to the nightly pipeline, found the same growth within hours on the next release candidate. The team fixed the missing close call and added an alert on descriptor use above seventy percent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Load tests with unrealistic traffic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No long running tests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fault injection without a stop condition\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Gates that are slow and bypassed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing only the happy path\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"test-types","text":"Test types"},{"id":"load-tests","text":"Load tests"},{"id":"soak-tests","text":"Soak tests"},{"id":"fault-injection","text":"Fault injection"},{"id":"gates-and-canaries","text":"Gates and canaries"},{"id":"incident-the-leak-that-only-showed-on-day-three","text":"Incident: The leak that only showed on day three"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Testing for reliability","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/testing-reliability/"},{"t":"Canarying releases","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/"},{"t":"Chaos engineering principles","u":"https://principlesofchaos.org/"}],"text":"Reliability that is only tested in production is tested by users. Many failure modes can be found earlier with repeatable tests that run for every release. What you will learn What each test type finds Load tests with realistic traffic shapes Soak tests for leaks and slow decay Fault injection: latency, errors and dependency loss Release gates and canary analysis Test types A load test shows how the service behaves at expected and peak traffic. A stress test finds the breaking point. A soak test runs for hours or days to find leaks and slow decay. A fault injection test breaks a dependency or adds latency to check that fallbacks work. Each answers a different question, so choose by the risk. Load tests Use traffic that resembles production in mix and shape, not a single endpoint at constant rate. Replay anonymised traffic or model the real mix, including bursts. Measure latency percentiles and errors, as well as resource use, and compare against the last release. A trend over releases finds regressions early. Soak tests Run the service at steady load for a long time and watch memory, file descriptors, connection counts and latency over time. A slow rise points to a leak. Soak tests also catch problems with log rotation, certificate renewal and scheduled jobs that appear only after hours. Fault injection Add delay or errors to a dependency call, stop a replica, or block a network path, and check that timeouts, retries, circuit breakers and fallbacks do what the design says. Begin in staging, then run small experiments in production with a clear stop condition. Record the hypothesis, the result and the fix. Gates and canaries Use the SLO signals as gates: a canary release receives a small share of traffic, and the pipeline compares its error rate and latency with the baseline. If it is worse, the release stops or rolls back automatically. Keep the gate fast and the thresholds simple, so that people trust it and do not bypass it. Testing resilience in pipelines: load, soak and fault injection Flow in order: 1, Pipeline to Load and soak; 2, Load and soak to Fault injection; 3, Fault injection to Canary gate. Step Observability Security Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Pipeline Step What it does: Every release. If it fails or is weak: Skipped. Load and soak Observability What it does: Shape and time. If it fails or is weak: Unrealistic. Fault injection Security What it does: Break a dependency. If it fails or is weak: No fallback. Canary gate Service What it does: SLO comparison. If it fails or is weak: Bypassed. Incident: The leak that only showed on day three Incident A service that passed every test ran out of file descriptors three days after release. $ ls /proc/$(pgrep -o app)/fd | wc -l 31872 $ ulimit -n 32768 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The tests ran for ten minutes and the leak of one descriptor per slow request needed days to show. A soak test of twelve hours at steady load, which the team added to the nightly pipeline, found the same growth within hours on the next release candidate. The team fixed the missing close call and added an alert on descriptor use above seventy percent. Common mistakes Load tests with unrealistic traffic No long running tests Fault injection without a stop condition Gates that are slow and bypassed Testing only the happy path"},"reliability-practice/the-reliability-programme-reviews-roadmaps-and-reporting":{"html":"\u003cp>Individual fixes help, but a programme makes reliability work visible, prioritised and funded. This final lesson joins the earlier practices into a yearly rhythm.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Production readiness reviews and launch checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A risk register built from incidents and near misses\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A reliability roadmap with funding\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reporting: a one page view for leaders\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Showing value: fewer incidents, faster recovery, happier users\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"readiness-reviews\">Readiness reviews\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before a service takes real traffic, review it against a checklist: SLOs and alerts exist, runbooks are written, capacity and failure behaviour have been tested, backups restore, ownership is clear and dependencies are known. Treat the review as help and not as a gate to punish. Record exceptions with an owner and a date to close them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"risk-register\">Risk register\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep a list of known risks with likelihood, impact and an owner. Feed it from post incident reviews, near misses, game days and audits. Rank by the product of impact and likelihood, and review it monthly. A risk that is accepted should be accepted by a named person who has the authority, with a date to look again.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roadmap\">Roadmap\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Turn the highest risks into planned work with estimates and put it on the same roadmap as features. Reserve capacity each quarter, for example fifteen to twenty percent, so that it is not squeezed out. Where the error budget policy has triggered, reliability work moves to the front of the queue.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reporting\">Reporting\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Give leaders one page each month: SLO status and budget for the main services, the biggest open risks, the incidents with their impact and the progress of the roadmap. Use trends and plain words. Avoid a long list of technical metrics, because leaders need to decide whether to fund work, not to debug it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"showing-value\">Showing value\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Track mean time to detect and to recover, the number of customer visible incidents, repeat incidents and pages per shift. Compare them each quarter. When a past incident type stops recurring because of a funded fix, say so. Visible results are what keeps reliability work funded in the next planning round.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"prr&gt;risk;risk&gt;road;road&gt;rep\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The reliability programme: reviews, roadmaps and reporting\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prr\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Readiness review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"risk\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Risk register\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"road\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Roadmap\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rep\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Leadership report\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Readiness review to Risk register; 2, Risk register to Roadmap; 3, Roadmap to Leadership report.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prr\">\u003cdt>Readiness review \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Checklist.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rubber stamp.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"risk\">\u003cdt>Risk register \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Ranked.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"road\">\u003cdt>Roadmap \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Funded work.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Squeezed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rep\">\u003cdt>Leadership report \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> One page.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too technical.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-same-outage-three-times-in-a-year\">Incident: The same outage three times in a year\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A certificate based outage recurred because the follow up work was never scheduled.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -c &quot;status: open&quot; postmortem-actions.csv\ngrep -c &quot;status: done&quot; postmortem-actions.csv\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status: open&quot;\u003c/span> postmortem-actions.csv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">27\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status: done&quot;\u003c/span> postmortem-actions.csv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Twenty seven actions from earlier reviews were still open and only four were done, because they had no owners and no place on the roadmap. After the third outage, leadership asked for a reliability programme. The team ranked the open actions as risks, reserved twenty percent of each quarter, assigned owners and reported progress monthly. The next year had no repeat of that outage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Readiness reviews as a rubber stamp\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Actions with no owner or date\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No reserved capacity for reliability\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reports full of technical metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never showing the result of fixes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"readiness-reviews","text":"Readiness reviews"},{"id":"risk-register","text":"Risk register"},{"id":"roadmap","text":"Roadmap"},{"id":"reporting","text":"Reporting"},{"id":"showing-value","text":"Showing value"},{"id":"incident-the-same-outage-three-times-in-a-year","text":"Incident: The same outage three times in a year"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Production readiness review","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/evolving-sre-engagement-model/"},{"t":"Postmortem culture","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/"},{"t":"SRE Workbook: error budget policy","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/error-budget-policy/"}],"text":"Individual fixes help, but a programme makes reliability work visible, prioritised and funded. This final lesson joins the earlier practices into a yearly rhythm. What you will learn Production readiness reviews and launch checks A risk register built from incidents and near misses A reliability roadmap with funding Reporting: a one page view for leaders Showing value: fewer incidents, faster recovery, happier users Readiness reviews Before a service takes real traffic, review it against a checklist: SLOs and alerts exist, runbooks are written, capacity and failure behaviour have been tested, backups restore, ownership is clear and dependencies are known. Treat the review as help and not as a gate to punish. Record exceptions with an owner and a date to close them. Risk register Keep a list of known risks with likelihood, impact and an owner. Feed it from post incident reviews, near misses, game days and audits. Rank by the product of impact and likelihood, and review it monthly. A risk that is accepted should be accepted by a named person who has the authority, with a date to look again. Roadmap Turn the highest risks into planned work with estimates and put it on the same roadmap as features. Reserve capacity each quarter, for example fifteen to twenty percent, so that it is not squeezed out. Where the error budget policy has triggered, reliability work moves to the front of the queue. Reporting Give leaders one page each month: SLO status and budget for the main services, the biggest open risks, the incidents with their impact and the progress of the roadmap. Use trends and plain words. Avoid a long list of technical metrics, because leaders need to decide whether to fund work, not to debug it. Showing value Track mean time to detect and to recover, the number of customer visible incidents, repeat incidents and pages per shift. Compare them each quarter. When a past incident type stops recurring because of a funded fix, say so. Visible results are what keeps reliability work funded in the next planning round. The reliability programme: reviews, roadmaps and reporting Flow in order: 1, Readiness review to Risk register; 2, Risk register to Roadmap; 3, Roadmap to Leadership report. Step Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Readiness review Step What it does: Checklist. If it fails or is weak: Rubber stamp. Risk register Data store What it does: Ranked. If it fails or is weak: Stale. Roadmap Step What it does: Funded work. If it fails or is weak: Squeezed. Leadership report Observability What it does: One page. If it fails or is weak: Too technical. Incident: The same outage three times in a year Incident A certificate based outage recurred because the follow up work was never scheduled. $ grep -c \"status: open\" postmortem-actions.csv 27 $ grep -c \"status: done\" postmortem-actions.csv 4 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Twenty seven actions from earlier reviews were still open and only four were done, because they had no owners and no place on the roadmap. After the third outage, leadership asked for a reliability programme. The team ranked the open actions as risks, reserved twenty percent of each quarter, assigned owners and reported progress monthly. The next year had no repeat of that outage. Common mistakes Readiness reviews as a rubber stamp Actions with no owner or date No reserved capacity for reliability Reports full of technical metrics Never showing the result of fixes"}});